Z||000005||theogre||05-31-2001||03:11 PM||||OE lamps are bretty bright most cars I've seen. I usually want to dim them. Especially on dark roads.<P>First thing to check,,, the socket on the dimmer transistor. If that's cruddy then the OE bulbs may not be on at full bright even with the control all the way up.<P>You need to disassemble the transistor, apply fresh heat sink grease (radio shack has it) and stuff it back together. The end effect is that you work the pins and scrub off oxide build up. Same effect as reseating CPU's or boards in a computer.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Ken Wittlief||05-31-2001||03:25 PM||||Ill have to find that transistor and try that - but my dash is pretty dim too - and I dont really care for the orange lights.<P>  I like the nice white dash on my saturns - esp when driving around town.<P>  so one of the things on my FieroDewList is to pull the dash and take the orange reflectors out, or put siver metal tape over them.<P>  the orange color reminds me of a dingy bar with the light bulbs covered with tar and nicotine.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Ken Wittlief||05-31-2001||03:39 PM||||I had a summer job when I was 16, helping a tavern owner clean his bar on Sunday mornings.<P>one of the things i did every week was get a bucket of water and soap, and wash the big plate glass front windows.<P> when I got done the bucket of soapy water was always about the color of Fiero dashboard backlites.<P>  Guess its a subconscious association thing with me.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Terrybogin||05-31-2001||07:31 PM||||On my son's Fiero, we removed the gauges and painted the white plastic backing with flourescent red spray paint. Then the stock bulbs were replaced with red 194's. The end result is very pleasing and no more bright orange needles at night!||||reg||1
Z||000009||Ken Wittlief||06-01-2001||10:14 AM||||ok I looked in the factory service manual and fond the transistor in the wiring diagram, but could not find where its located on the car.<P>  did notice that the inst lights fuse is after the transistor - so you should be able to put a voltmeter from the fuse to ground, and see if you are getting a full 12.8 V with the adjustment wheel all the way up - no point in pulling the transistor out and cleaning it if its already putting out the full 12.8V - another thing on my FieroDewList to check.||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||06-01-2001||01:37 PM||||That's not an entirely accurate test. With the fuse out the transistor may show 12+V but that's not the only thing going on. Remember, you have to get full current accross the transistor, not just voltage. You could see ful voltage but still not pass enough current.<P>There are a couple things involved. <P>One is a cruddy "base" terminal. this one keeps the transistor from turning all the way on. This may or maynot show a voltage drop at the fuse but it will chop current to the lamps.<P>The other is a very slight voltage drop across either the "collector" or the "emitter" terminal. In this case the transistor may appear to be fully on in a test as described above but the small voltage drop across the the dirty terminal can really chop Current to the lamps. This is why I recomended pulling the transistor for cleaning.<P>I think to see the transistor easily, you have to take out the plastic panel under the steering column. As you face up I think It's to your left of the steering column. It's been awhile since I looked, and I can't crawl under there to check for a few more weeks.<P>A slightly better test may be to leave the fuse in and use the test hole in the top of the fuse to measure the voltage. this leaves the lamps as a load on the transistor. That might give you a better idea if you can compaire a known working car to yours... Even then I'm not sure you'll get an accurate test since the diferant bulbs can draw slightly diferant current.<P><BR>Also,,, have you tried simply putting fresh lamps in and cleaning the holders? Old lamps can build up "soot" on the inside as they age. That will make them dim. A small amount of the soot can have a big impact on lamp brightness. Dirty/damaged holders will cut brightness simply becuase the lamp won't get full power. Check the little arms that contact the dash traces. Those have a nasty habit of cracking and making lame connection.||||reg||1
Z||000011||DecadenceR||06-01-2001||03:28 PM||||I know the lights in my 84 are pretty dim.<BR>But I just figured that was because it's not backlit.||||reg||1
